>>It's not cheating but it's pretty damned weak.
>It occurs to me, David: you've made the point on a number of occasions 
>that NetHack is *not* a roleplaying game. It's purely strategy.

That's true, yes.

However, I think there are certain strategies that ought not to work; as
in, that some combination of oversight on the Dev Team's part and them not
having got around to fixing it yet. In particular, in this case almost
everyone does seem to shun the HoOA strategy even though it is normally
easy to pull off, which suggests the impetus to fix it is small.

Basically I suppose the game has a certain intended challenge which some
techniques short-circuit, and I spurn those. Obviously it's speculation -
I don't have any definite opinion and maybe HoOA shuffles are actually
meant to work - but it seems unlikely.

Also, one can desire internal consistency without doing any roleplaying,
and it seems to me that the idea of the character ascending and then
presumably keeping the HoOA on for eternity is faintly absurd. It's as if
there was a level in Pac-man where you had tea with the ghosts - there's
no roleplaying in Pac-man but it still would be silly.
